Note:
# LM hash (VERY outdated and bad)
# NT hash (Hashes of local accounts. In SAM. Can be used in PtH. Can also be cracked.) #TODO, does it also store network account hashes? 
# ASNWERED DOES NOT STORE NETWORK ACCOUNT HASHES
# SAM stored hash has LM:NT stored as both hashes. The LM hash is generic and same
# NetNTLMv1 and NetNTLMv2 hash (Hash used during NTLM auth over network, can't be used for PtH, can still be cracked. Can be relayed). 
# NetNTLMv1 hashes are older and easier to crack. v2 uses HMAC-MD5 to hash
# v1 uses the WAY MORE outdated and is DES-based: the 16-byte NT hash is split into three 7-byte chunks, each used as a DES key to encrypt the 8-byte challenge, producing a 24-byte response
# Aka leaks the hash accidentally
# Domain cached credentials (DCC/DCC2, formerly "mscache") live in the SECURITY hive, not SAM. They're a different format entirely—mscash/mscash2, crackable with hashcat -m 1100/-m 2100.
# These can't be used in PTH, but can be found with secretsdump
# SAM = local accounts. SECURITY hive = cached domain creds. Nowhere = NetNTLM (computed per-auth)

Clearer notes:
