The naive solution: Each service stores your password hash and authenticates you directly. This is what MySQL does. But it doesn't scale:

Every service must store every user's credentials (or delegate to a central database, which is just moving the problem).

Every service must be trusted with your credentials.

A compromise of any service compromises all credentials.

Kerberos's solution: A trusted third party (the KDC) authenticates you once and issues tickets that prove your identity to any service. The services trust the KDC, so they trust the tickets.

